speak now to the leader of the labour  party sir keir starmak joining us from a  little bit further north secure lovely  to see you this morning um i hope you  were able just to listen into to a  little of what we were hearing from from  the government this morning in terms of  their response to the cost of living  crisis but can we be brutally honest no  matter whether it's a conservative prime  minister a labour prime minister monster  raving looney prime minister in downing  street at the moment no politician can  act to ameliorate all of the effects of  the cost of living crisis  well of course it's a difficult  situation i think we all appreciate that  but  you know today is a really significant  and worrying day  for millions of people because their  energy bills are going to go up by  hundreds of pounds and i've spoken to  people across the country  particularly those who are struggling  financially and they're really worried  about tossing and turning in their beds  about how they're going to pay for this  what we've said as the labour party is  look oil and gas companies in the north  sea have made more profit than they  expected because global prices have been  high therefore we should have a windfall  tax on that and use it to reduce those  energy bills by up to 600 pounds for  those that need it most so there's a  very practical plan on the table the  only plan on the table quite frankly  we've also said of all the times for the  government to decide to impose more tax  in national insurance now is the wrong  time it's the wrong tax it's the wrong  time and so  um i think  most people  are clear that when  you know the government really needed to  step up in that spring statement last  week they failed to do so and their  response is frankly pathetic on a very  very difficult day for millions and  millions of people energy bills going up  for you know more than we've ever seen  on record  you know people are really struggling  and i just don't think the government  gets it  yeah  just just though on the on the windfall  tax plan i mean this isn't the problem  yeah that was identified by pat mcfadden  your colleague when he was speaking to  me last week you can do it once so your  words are really important right now you  haven't said it in this interview but  you have said previously the windfall  tax plan would all but eliminates the  energy price rise for low-income  families pensioners and middle-income  families i mean  that might be true  now but it won't be true later on this  year when we see another set of price  rises  look it is a one-off tax um it's a  windfall tax but it would deal with the  immediate crisis and people waking up  today are asking the question  uh is there a plan on the table to deal  with the problem i've got right now  which is this rise in my prices uh  energy costs of hundreds of pounds and  they're seeing nothing from the  government the government says well you  can have a loan starting in october of  about two hundred pounds so six months  ago  this be paid back but my point is simply  this yeah  my point is simply this yeah the  windfall tanks that you're proposing  will give a break of a hunt a few  hundred pounds right now which will  frankly by my looking at the numbers be  utterly wiped out by the price rise that  we're all expecting come october  well there are two answers to that the  first is obviously there's got to be a  wide and medium a long-term strategy and  that's why we set out our energy  strategy last week which included  turbocharging renewables  fast forwarding on nuclear power but  importantly and significantly also  a project  to insulate homes and make them warmer  and i saw i'm up in manchester at the  moment last week i was in kirkleese and  i saw for myself council houses that  have already gone through that process  of insulation and i met the residents  there their bills are already very very  low because of that insulation program  led by a labour council that's the sort  of medium to long-term measure we need  so the labour party says we've got a  plan for dealing with the immediate  crisis  which is facing people today but we've  also got a plan for the longer term the  other thing i would add of course is  that you know as global prices stay high  that profit for the oil and gas  companies will be even higher probably  by the autumn than it is now but  contrast the labour party that's got a  practical plan to reduce bills now and  the government's pathetic response they  just don't get it they don't realize how  hard people are struggling just at the  moment  for a variety of reasons  with the government's plan to increase  tax and do nothing about these energy  prices okay but what in term okay in  terms then of benefits why does labour  not agree with with plenty of those  working uh you know in ngos at the  moment that benefits should not be  raised in line with inflation  well i think that that is something we  should look at because uh benefits have  fallen behind what what people wanted  from the government last week in that  spring state no no no no no i'm asking  you i'm asking you would you  as a point of principle would you  should look at  yeah would you i think  it's not it's not enough to say you're  going to look at i'm asking for a policy  commitment here would you increase in  line with inflation because if you don't  people on benefits experience a real  terms cut in the amount amount of money  that they have  yeah i think we've got to look at it for  that very reason because otherwise  people fall behind that's what we wanted  to see from the government last week  that's what we didn't see from the  government last week frankly if the  government put a package on the table  that parliament could agree we could get  on with this  but the government just doesn't get the  scale of the problem for millions of  millions of people  local elections coming up in five weeks  time also um and if people want to send  a message to the government to say um  britain deserves better than this your  response to the cost of living crisis is  pathetic this is the chance to do it  what labor is saying we've got a plan on  the table and we're we're on your side  we understand we take your worries  seriously  what about the minimum wage the rise  we're seeing today is is apparently the  third largest rise since the crash of  2008 some people are asking why it's not  the largest you know price the increase  in minimum wage that we've seen since  since 2008. it wasn't that long ago that  andy mcdonald resigned from the front  bench because you wouldn't agree to a  minimum wage of 15 pounds an hour i mean  isn't that something that the labour  party could as a progressive party get  behind right now that would have an  immediate effect  on the quality of life for many many  people  yeah what we put on the table at our  conference was an increase in the  minimum wage to  about 10 pounds as part of a package of  employment rights day one rights and i  can tell you this had that package been  in place had you had a labor government  taking seriously the security of people  at work you would not have had the  scandal of piano sucking 800 people in  the awful way that we they did the  government was on notice about that not  just because they had the memo the night  before the 800 were made redundant but  also because two years ago  we were saying to them if you don't act  on this minimum wage for seafarers  a company in the end is going to crash  through the gate and reduce  their replace their workforce with  agency workers on less than the minimum  wage that's exactly what has happened  two years ago the government said oh we  acknowledge that's a problem we'll look  at it okay they did nothing pno crashed  through the gate 800 people lost their  british public jobs that is is huge  so so do you generally believe the  british public are looking at what  labour is offering in contrast to the  government at the moment i i i'm  thinking this is a radical party this is  a party with big ideas an increase to  roughly 10 pounds and kind of  for the for the minimum wage your  one-off windfall tax that would cover  most of your price increases right now  but nothing for later on in the year  where's the invention  what i want people to  do is to look at the labour party and  see a party that understands their  worries that takes them  seriously and is prepared to put  practical plans on the paper on the  table people don't want a revolution  they do want to know how am i going to  pay my energy bill which has just gone  up today by hundreds of pounds we're  putting a practical plan on the paper on  the table it says for those that need it  most  you ask oil and gas companies who have  made more profit than they were  expecting to make because of the high  global prices we say ask those companies  to pay their fair share and that would  reduce those bills by 600 pounds so when  i'm talking to pensioners i was in  stevenage last week talking to  pensioners they weren't saying here we  want the revolution they were saying  here we really worried about our bill  one of the pensioners said to me that  they keep their heating on 12 degrees  because they think if they put it higher  than that  they can't afford the bill and this you  know people having to make a choice  between heating and eating i mean in  21st century britain what people want to  know is is the labour party does it  understand those worries and the answer  that is yes we do have we got a  practical solution to put on the table  to reduce those bills by up to 600  pounds yes we have contrast that with  the government where there was just a  pathetic response which isn't going to  help very many people at all and this is  the thing that people are talking about  all over the country and the question in  the end is  you know which party is on your side and  the labour party's on your side i just  want to conclude if we can by talking  about events in inverted commas and  around downing street carrie johnson you  seem to have suggested it should be  named if she is fined over partygate and  if that's the case should we then expect  to hear you know the next time your wife  gets a parking ticket  i think there's a huge difference  between um the situation of the wife of  the prime minister breaking the rules  made by the prime minister and any other  situation and i'm i'm not focusing on um  carrie here i actually do agree with the  general argument that the families of  politicians shouldn't be dragged in but  in and and therefore i focus  well rishi soon axe wife again i agree i  do agree with the general proposition  that  you know family members shouldn't be  brought into political arguments but so  far as the chancellor's wife is  concerned this is just a fundamental  question of principle here which is is  their household benefiting from money  made in russia when the government's put  in place sanctions that is in the public  interest for us to have an answer to i'm  not attacking um their family um i don't  agree with that way of doing politics  but i do want to know is the chanceless  household the chancellor's household  benefiting from money from a company  that's investing in russia when the  government is saying quite rightly  but nobody should be doing that  sanctions should be in place and we  should particularly in the dreadful  situation in ukraine frankly be doing  everything we can not just to  make life more difficult in russia but  to [ __ ] their ability to function so  it's not a personal attack i don't agree  with that i absolutely don't agree with  that but i do think that i would have  thought the chance would actually want  to come clean on this and say actually i  can be very very clear that my household  doesn't benefit from any money that's  come in any way from russia  during this invasion of ukraine it's a  simple question i think you should just  answer it actually help his wife have  you just answered that question  um secure stamina we have to leave it  there because i have to admit it sounds  like the breakfast rush might be  starting just just over your shoulder  there secure stamina thanks very much  for joining us appreciate it  thank you

